<section id="install" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">A Go, ready in three steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps-grid steps-grid--sequence">
    <li>
      <h3>Order.</h3>
      <p>Order a LilyGO T-Echo or T-Echo Plus, in 868&nbsp;MHz with the BME280 barometer.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Install.</h3>
      <p>Plug the T-Echo into a computer with a USB-C cable. In Chrome or Edge, open <%= link_to "Set up a device", briefing_setup_path %> in Briefing, the skyblip.eu web app, and click Install. Briefing restarts the T-Echo as a USB drive named <span class="mono">TECHOBOOT</span>, writes skyBlip onto it, then connects to it over Bluetooth.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Activate.</h3>
      <p>Activation runs by itself once Briefing connects. The Go transmits in ADS-L, so activation assigns it an address from skyBlip&rsquo;s range in the ADS-L registry. No account is needed. The Go trusts the browser that installed it, so it asks for no press. Until then, a new Go neither transmits nor receives. Briefing then lets you set your <a href="#type">aircraft type</a> and callsign. Both are optional. If the device needs a GPS position before saving them, it tells you. You can change both later in the device&rsquo;s menu or from <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Without Chrome or Edge on a computer</summary>
      <p>Connect the device by USB-C and press <span class="mono">RESET</span> twice. Copy the .uf2 file from the <a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/releases">latest release</a> to the <span class="mono">TECHOBOOT</span> drive that appears. Then <%= link_to "activate it", briefing_activation_path %> over Bluetooth in Chrome or Edge on Android, and confirm activation with a press on the device.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="install-bravo" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">A Bravo, ready in three steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps-grid steps-grid--sequence">
    <li>
      <h3>Order.</h3>
      <p>Order a MeshTracker X1.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Install.</h3>
      <p>Plug the X1 into a computer with a USB-C cable. In Chrome or Edge, open <%= link_to "Set up a device", briefing_setup_path %> in Briefing and click Install. Briefing restarts the X1 as a USB drive named <span class="mono">X1</span>, writes skyBlip onto it, then connects to it over Bluetooth.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Set your transponder address.</h3>
      <p>Only a device that transmits in ADS-L needs its own address from skyBlip&rsquo;s range in the ADS-L registry. The Go gets it at activation, while Bravo transmits nothing and needs neither activation nor an account.</p>
      <p>If your aircraft has a Mode S or ADS-B out transponder, Bravo must know its ICAO address, so your own transponder stays out of the traffic and sets off no alarm. Nothing to type: in the aircraft, switch the transponder to <span class="mono">ALT</span>. With a Go paired, the Go offers the address, the callsign and the type Bravo hears, and one press on the Go takes them. With Bravo alone, open <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>: it offers the address Bravo hears. If you know the address already, such as <span class="mono">3C6586</span>, you can enter it at setup or later in the device&rsquo;s settings. Without a transponder, there is nothing to set.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Without Chrome or Edge on a computer</summary>
      <p>Hold the button, plug in the USB-C cable and keep holding until the LED turns white, about 3 seconds. A drive named <span class="mono">X1</span> appears. First, copy <span class="mono">CURRENT.UF2</span> from it to your computer. Dragging that file back onto the drive restores the firmware the X1 shipped with. Then copy the .uf2 file of the <a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/releases">latest skyBlip Bravo release</a> onto the <span class="mono">X1</span> drive.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
  <p class="signature">
    A Go and a Bravo set up in the same browser pair with one click in
    <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>, and with no press. Later updates for both devices
    come from Briefing over Bluetooth. A browser that installed a device updates it with no press.
    Any other browser asks for one: on the Go&rsquo;s screen, or on Bravo&rsquo;s button.
    The same file works on the T&#8209;Echo and the T&#8209;Echo Plus. For now,
    only development versions are published for both devices.
  </p>
</section>

